1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,294
Host: Welcome to KC Desk Notes. Today we are unpacking one fresh research report and the question it raises.

2
00:00:05,654 --> 00:00:14,060
Host: Welcome back to the show. Today we’re looking at a new report from Nomura that tracks a sharp slowdown in China’s domestic auto sales for May.

3
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:22,268
Guest: That’s right. The headline number is a decline of about 21.6% year-on-year in domestic passenger car retail sales volume for May.

4
00:00:22,628 --> 00:00:25,043
Host: That’s a pretty steep drop. What’s driving this?

5
00:00:25,403 --> 00:00:30,883
Guest: The report points to a couple of key factors. One is a payback effect from the scaled-back trade-in program.

6
00:00:31,243 --> 00:00:35,283
Guest: The other is the rise in the EV purchase tax from zero to 5%.

7
00:00:35,643 --> 00:00:42,423
Host: So the policy side incentives that were boosting sales are now being unwound, and that’s hitting demand hard.

8
00:00:42,783 --> 00:00:50,353
Guest: Exactly. The domestic market is acting as a major drag. And the report notes that the auto sector accounts for over 3% of China’s GDP,

9
00:00:50,713 --> 00:00:52,710
Guest: so this matters for the broader economy.

10
00:00:53,070 --> 00:00:57,017
Host: But I know exports have been a bright spot. Is that helping to offset the weakness?

11
00:00:57,377 --> 00:01:04,715
Guest: Yes, exports are growing very fast. In April, export volumes were up 84% year-on-year, accelerating from 60% in Q1.

12
00:01:05,075 --> 00:01:07,258
Host: That’s a huge jump. What’s behind that surge?

13
00:01:07,618 --> 00:01:13,887
Guest: The report mentions rising oil and gas prices globally, which is driving demand for Chinese EVs. Also,

14
00:01:14,247 --> 00:01:18,287
Guest: energy disruptions are giving Chinese carmakers some unique advantages.

15
00:01:18,647 --> 00:01:22,827
Host: So the export story is strong, but can it fully compensate for the domestic slump?

16
00:01:23,187 --> 00:01:30,292
Guest: Not quite. The report notes that exports only account for about 19% of volume and 15% of value in China’s total auto market.

17
00:01:30,652 --> 00:01:33,764
Host: So the domestic market is still the much larger piece of the pie.

18
00:01:34,124 --> 00:01:42,204
Guest: Correct. And that’s why the report emphasizes a widening divide between external and domestic demand, which they expect to persist through 2026.

19
00:01:42,564 --> 00:01:46,512
Host: That’s a really interesting framework. So we have a two-speed auto market in China.

20
00:01:46,872 --> 00:01:54,488
Guest: Exactly. The export engine is running hot, but the domestic engine is cooling off. And the report suggests that trend is likely to continue.

21
00:01:54,848 --> 00:02:01,628
Host: One thing I’m curious about—the report doesn’t fully settle what happens next if trade tensions or tariffs escalate.

22
00:02:01,988 --> 00:02:09,651
Guest: That’s a good point. The report focuses on the current data and the structural divide, but it doesn’t model out a full trade-war scenario.

23
00:02:10,011 --> 00:02:13,122
Guest: That’s definitely something worth digging into in the full report.

24
00:02:13,482 --> 00:02:21,655
Host: So for listeners who want to understand the full picture, including the potential risks to the export story, it’s worth reading the original research.

25
00:02:22,015 --> 00:02:26,474
Guest: Absolutely. There’s a lot more detail on the month-to-month data and the underlying assumptions.

26
00:02:26,834 --> 00:02:33,521
Host: Great. Thanks for breaking that down. We’ll leave it there for now, but feel free to continue the discussion in our community.

27
00:02:33,881 --> 00:02:34,949
Guest: Thanks for having me.
